{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787236366,"nonce":"E4WTZUvzi7NR16SuvEDHOyrH1Do=","title":"**What is wrong.** When a Slack member who has already pressed buttons later links a real oto account,","message":"`LinkSlackIdentity` re-points the `slack_identities` row and retires the shadow member (git-bug\n`a74d6b2`, migration `00074`) — but every row the shadow already authored still names the shadow's\nuuid. So one human reads as two members in the history: the acks they made before linking are\nattributed to a retired shadow, and the ones after to their real account.\n\n**The demonstration.** The shadow is the principal on four foreign keys plus their denormalised\nlabels: `alert_cases.acked_by`, `alert_snoozes.snoozed_by`, `alert_snoozes.ended_by` and\n`delivery_drills.started_by`, alongside the `*_by_label` columns that cached the actor's display name\nat write time, and the actor recorded inside `alert_events` payloads. `RetireShadow` sets\n`disabled_at`; it rewrites none of them. A Case acked before the link and resolved after it therefore\nshows two different actors for one person.\n\n**Why it matters.** The timeline is the audit record oto sells, and §A's whole claim is that it never\nlies. \"Two members did this\" is a false statement about who acted, and it is unfalsifiable from the\nUI — nothing on the screen says the two rows are the same human. It is also silent: nothing warns at\nlink time that history is about to fork.\n\n**Why it is not urgent.** `LinkSlackIdentity` has **no production caller** — no contract operation\nlinks a Slack identity today — so the conflict is latent rather than live. The adoption logic was\nwritten in `a74d6b2` anyway, precisely so the feature could not ship as a silent duplicate the day\nthat caller appears. This ticket is the other half, and it should land before the linking route does.\n\n**Where.** `internal/identity/service` (`LinkSlackIdentity`, the adoption path) ·\n`internal/identity/repository/users.go` (`RetireShadow`) · `alert_cases.acked_by` ·\n`alert_snoozes.snoozed_by` / `ended_by` · `delivery_drills.started_by` · the `*_by_label` columns ·\n`alert_events` payload actors · `db/migrations/00074_a_shadow_member_has_no_address.sql`.\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** Linking a Slack identity to a real account leaves exactly one actor in the history: the\nfour FKs and their cached labels are re-pointed from the shadow to the adopted user in one\ntransaction, and the `alert_events` payload actors are reconciled or explicitly ruled out of scope\nwith the reason. A test acks as an unlinked member, links, acks again, and asserts both events name\none member. Note this is a data migration over existing rows as well as a service change — an\ninstallation that already has shadow acks needs the backfill, and the migration must say what it does\nto rows whose shadow was never adopted.","files":null}]}